{
  "title": "Concurrent single-game launches silently collide into one game dir",
  "description": "batch_coordination.py names game dirs game_<timestamp> where \
the timestamp has second resolution, is minted AFTER Xvfb/JVM startup, and the \
_g<n> suffix only disambiguates within one process's batch. Parallel --games 1 \
launches that reach the mint in the same second therefore share a directory, \
and mkdir(exist_ok=True) makes the collision silent: both games write into it \
and any consumer that collects by dir name reads one game's episode for both. \
Measured 2026-08-23 on a 20-way-parallel RL rollout driver: 429 launches \
produced 97 distinct dirs; one dir was claimed by 11 launches spanning 4 \
experimental arms, and training batches of 20 rows held 3-4 distinct episodes \
with wrong group labels. A launch stagger does NOT fix it because startup \
latency varies enough to reconverge on the same second; a startup lock \
serialising mint-to-mkdir does, but removing the shared namespace is better. \
MAGEBENCH_LOG_DIR (added with this issue) lets a driver give each launch a \
private namespace, which the RL driver now does. The default namespace is \
still collision-prone for anyone else. Proper fix: make the dir name unique by \
construction (pid + monotonic counter or uuid suffix), and drop exist_ok so a \
collision fails loudly instead of interleaving. Related: the timestamp is \
rendered in ZoneInfo(America/Los_Angeles) regardless of host timezone, so on \
this UTC-4 host every dir name is 3h behind wall clock -- any provenance \
reasoning from the name is wrong by 3h, which misdirected one debugging \
session already.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 1,
  "type": "bug",
  "labels": [
    "orchestration",
